window.STQA_CONTENT = {
  units: [
    {id:1,title:"Basic Concepts and Preliminaries",subtitle:"Build the foundations of software quality and testing.",icon:"◈",topics:[
      {id:"u1-quality-revolution",title:"Quality Revolution",summary:"How software quality thinking evolved",body:`
<h2>Start with the idea</h2><p>Imagine a college attendance application. Its interface may look attractive, but if it loses attendance records or crashes during class, it is not a quality product. Quality must be built into the way software is planned, designed, developed, checked and maintained—not inspected only at the very end.</p>
<h2>What is the Quality Revolution?</h2><p>The quality revolution describes the shift from relying mainly on final-product inspection and defect correction toward preventing defects, improving the development process continuously, and treating quality as a responsibility shared across the organization.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Earlier approach</th><th>Quality-focused approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inspect the product at the end.</td><td>Build checks into every development stage.</td></tr><tr><td>Find and repair defects after they occur.</td><td>Prevent defects through clear requirements, reviews and standards.</td></tr><tr><td>Quality is mainly the tester's job.</td><td>Developers, testers, managers and stakeholders share responsibility.</td></tr><tr><td>Count defects as the only signal.</td><td>Consider customer needs, reliability, risk and process performance.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Core principles</h2><ul><li><b>Defect prevention:</b> clarify requirements, review designs and code, and train the team before defects spread.</li><li><b>Continuous improvement:</b> use evidence from reviews, tests and production feedback to improve the process repeatedly.</li><li><b>Customer focus:</b> judge quality by whether the software meets genuine user needs.</li><li><b>Shared responsibility:</b> quality is created by the whole team, not added by a tester at the end.</li></ul>
<div class="callout green"><b>Example:</b> Reviewing an attendance calculation requirement before coding can prevent a whole class of mistakes. A final test may discover the mistake later, but prevention usually costs less than correcting a defect after release.</div>
<h2>Exam-ready answer</h2><p>The quality revolution changed software development from a predominantly defect-detection approach to a process-oriented approach emphasizing prevention, customer satisfaction, team responsibility and continuous improvement. These principles improve dependability and reduce the cost and impact of defects.</p>
<h2>Check your understanding</h2><p>Why is testing at the end alone insufficient? Because errors introduced in requirements or design may spread through the product, become expensive to correct, and remain undetected if test coverage is incomplete.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-software-quality",title:"Software Quality",summary:"What makes software fit for its intended use",body:`
<h2>Definition</h2><p><b>Software quality</b> is the degree to which a software product satisfies stated requirements and user needs under specified conditions. Quality is multidimensional: correctness alone does not guarantee that a product is usable, secure, efficient or maintainable.</p>
<h2>Quality characteristics in practice</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Attendance-app example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Correctness</td><td>Produces results consistent with requirements.</td><td>Attendance percentages are calculated accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Operates without failure for a specified time and context.</td><td>Records are not lost during normal use.</td></tr><tr><td>Usability</td><td>Users can understand and operate it effectively.</td><td>Students can find the attendance button easily.</td></tr><tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>Uses time and computing resources appropriately.</td><td>The attendance page loads quickly.</td></tr><tr><td>Security / integrity</td><td>Protects information from unauthorized access or change.</td><td>A student cannot alter another student's record.</td></tr><tr><td>Maintainability</td><td>Can be modified and repaired without excessive effort.</td><td>A developer can safely fix a percentage-calculation defect.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Why zero defects is not the whole story</h2><p>A program might produce mathematically correct results but be so confusing that users cannot complete their work. It might also be slow or expose private data. Software quality therefore combines conformance to requirements with fitness for actual use and other important quality attributes.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Remember:</b> Quality means doing the right job correctly, dependably, safely and in a way that meets users' needs.</div>
<h2>Exam practice</h2><p><b>Question:</b> Define software quality and explain four characteristics with examples.</p><p><b>Answer structure:</b> Give the definition, explain four characteristics, provide one example for each, and conclude that quality has several dimensions.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-role-testing",title:"Role of Testing",summary:"Evidence, risk reduction and release decisions",body:`
<h2>What testing does</h2><p>Software testing evaluates software and related work products to find defects and provide information about whether requirements and user needs are satisfied. It helps teams make better decisions about product quality and release risk.</p><ul><li>Detect defects before users encounter them.</li><li>Check business rules and expected behavior.</li><li>Reduce the likelihood and impact of failures.</li><li>Provide evidence for release and acceptance decisions.</li><li>Build confidence in the product.</li></ul>
<h2>Example: shopping application</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Test action</th><th>Expected outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Add one item to the cart.</td><td>The cart contains the correct item and quantity.</td></tr><tr><td>Enter an incorrect password.</td><td>Login is rejected and a clear message is shown.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply an expired coupon.</td><td>No invalid discount is applied.</td></tr><tr><td>Lose connectivity during payment.</td><td>The application handles the interruption safely and avoids duplicate charging.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Important limitation</h2><p>Testing can demonstrate the presence of defects, but passing tests does not generally prove that complex software contains no defects. Confidence depends on the quality of the tests, coverage, assumptions and risks considered.</p>
<div class="callout green"><b>Exam keyword:</b> Testing reduces risk and supplies information; it does not guarantee perfection.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-vv",title:"Verification and Validation",summary:"Are we building it right—and the right product?",body:`
<h2>Definitions</h2><p><b>Verification</b> checks whether work products conform to specified requirements and design. It asks: <em>“Are we building the product right?”</em></p><p><b>Validation</b> checks whether the resulting product satisfies intended use and user needs. It asks: <em>“Are we building the right product?”</em></p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Verification</th><th>Validation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main question</td><td>Does the work product meet its specification?</td><td>Does the product meet user needs?</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Requirements, design, code and other artifacts.</td><td>Behavior of the product in intended use.</td></tr><tr><td>Typical activities</td><td>Reviews, inspections, static analysis.</td><td>Executing tests, acceptance testing, usability evaluation.</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Review that the attendance formula matches the written specification.</td><td>Confirm that teachers can record attendance in the actual workflow.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>How they work together</h2><p>Verification can catch ambiguity or inconsistency before implementation. Validation checks whether the delivered solution actually solves the intended problem. Both are necessary because a product can follow a flawed specification perfectly and still fail its users.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Memory trick:</b> Verification = specification conformance. Validation = intended use and user needs.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-fault-error-failure",title:"Failure, Error, Fault and Defect",summary:"Distinguish human mistakes, flaws and observed failures",body:`
<h2>Understand the chain</h2><p>These terms are related but not interchangeable. A person may make an <b>error</b> (a human action or misconception); that can introduce a <b>fault</b> or defect into a requirement, design, code or other artifact. When the faulty software executes under particular conditions, it may produce an incorrect internal state and an externally observable <b>failure</b>.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Error</td><td>A human mistake or incorrect action/decision; terminology can vary by standard.</td><td>A developer misunderstands a requirement.</td></tr><tr><td>Fault / defect</td><td>A flaw in a work product that may cause incorrect behavior.</td><td>The code uses the wrong divisor in an attendance calculation.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>Observed inability of the software to perform a required function.</td><td>The app displays 125% attendance for a student.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Important nuance</h2><p>A fault does not always cause a failure. The relevant code may never execute, or the conditions needed to expose the fault may not occur. A failure is the visible manifestation; the underlying fault is what investigation seeks to locate.</p>
<div class="callout green"><b>Exam tip:</b> Explain the relationship and give one connected example rather than writing three unrelated definitions.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-reliability",title:"Software Reliability",summary:"Dependable operation over time and under stated conditions",body:`
<h2>Meaning</h2><p>Software reliability concerns the probability that software performs without failure for a specified period in a specified environment and under stated operating conditions. Always state the conditions and time interval when discussing reliability.</p>
<h2>What influences reliability?</h2><ul><li><b>Faults in the software:</b> defects in requirements, design, code or configuration.</li><li><b>Operational profile:</b> the frequency with which different features and input types are used.</li><li><b>Execution environment:</b> hardware, operating system, network and dependent services.</li><li><b>Input conditions:</b> normal, boundary, unexpected or invalid inputs.</li><li><b>Change and maintenance:</b> modifications can remove defects but may also introduce new ones.</li><li><b>Testing and prevention:</b> effective reviews and representative tests reduce residual risk.</li></ul>
<h2>Reliability versus quality</h2><p>Reliability is one important aspect of quality, but quality also includes usability, security, performance and maintainability. A system may be reliable yet difficult to use.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Example:</b> If a service is expected to run continuously during college registration, repeated crashes during peak registration indicate poor reliability under that operational profile.</div>
<h2>Exam-ready structure</h2><p>Define reliability; mention probability, failure-free operation, specified time and conditions; explain influencing factors; finish with a relevant example.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-objectives",title:"Objectives of Testing",summary:"Why teams test software",body:`
<h2>Main objectives</h2><ol><li><b>Detect defects:</b> expose faults before delivery.</li><li><b>Verify requirements:</b> check conformance to specifications.</li><li><b>Validate user needs:</b> determine whether the product supports intended tasks.</li><li><b>Reduce risk:</b> focus effort on failures with high likelihood or impact.</li><li><b>Provide information:</b> communicate quality, coverage and unresolved issues.</li><li><b>Support decisions:</b> help stakeholders decide whether to release, accept or investigate further.</li><li><b>Build confidence:</b> provide evidence that important behaviors have been checked.</li></ol>
<h2>Example</h2><p>Before releasing a fee-payment feature, testers check correct fee calculation, rejection of invalid payments, safe handling of network interruptions and prevention of duplicate transactions.</p>
<div class="callout">Testing is not simply “proving the software works.” It is a systematic way to discover problems and provide useful evidence about quality and risk.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-test-case",title:"What Is a Test Case?",summary:"Inputs, conditions, actions and expected results",body:`
<h2>Definition</h2><p>A <b>test case</b> is a documented set of preconditions, inputs, actions and expected results used to check a particular behavior or requirement.</p>
<h2>Typical elements</h2><ul><li>Test case ID and title.</li><li>Requirement or objective.</li><li>Preconditions and test environment.</li><li>Input data.</li><li>Execution steps.</li><li>Expected result.</li><li>Actual result and pass/fail status.</li></ul>
<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example: login test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ID</td><td>LOGIN-002</td></tr><tr><td>Precondition</td><td>User account exists and is active.</td></tr><tr><td>Input</td><td>Correct username, incorrect password.</td></tr><tr><td>Steps</td><td>Open login page, enter details, submit.</td></tr><tr><td>Expected result</td><td>Access is denied; a helpful error message appears.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Why expected results matter</h2><p>Without an expected result, a tester may observe behavior but cannot judge objectively whether it is correct. Expected outcomes should be determined before execution where possible.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-expected-outcome",title:"Expected Outcome",summary:"Define the oracle before executing a test",body:`
<h2>Meaning</h2><p>The expected outcome is the result or behavior that should occur for a given test input and execution condition. It is compared with the actual outcome to determine whether the test passes.</p><h2>Sources for expected outcomes</h2><ul><li>Requirements and acceptance criteria.</li><li>Design documents and interface contracts.</li><li>Business rules and domain constraints.</li><li>Reference implementations or independently calculated results.</li><li>Standards and documented user expectations.</li></ul><h2>Example</h2><p>If a fee calculator receives tuition ₹20,000 and a valid scholarship of ₹2,000, the expected payable amount is ₹18,000, assuming no other fees or rules apply.</p><div class="callout">A test is only as meaningful as its expected result. If the oracle is wrong or vague, the pass/fail judgment may also be wrong.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-central-issue",title:"Central Issue in Testing",summary:"Selecting tests that reveal important defects",body:`
<h2>The central challenge</h2><p>Testing every possible input and execution path is generally impractical for non-trivial software. The central issue is choosing a manageable set of test cases that is likely to reveal important defects and provide adequate confidence.</p><h2>Why exhaustive testing is difficult</h2><ul><li>Inputs may have enormous or infinite combinations.</li><li>Programs can have many paths, including loops.</li><li>Execution environments and states multiply combinations.</li><li>Time and budget are limited.</li></ul><h2>How test selection helps</h2><p>Testers use requirements, risk, equivalence classes, boundary values, structural coverage, operational profiles and past defect information to choose tests. No single criterion guarantees that all defects will be found.</p><div class="callout green"><b>Example:</b> For an age field accepting 18–60, test representative valid and invalid values plus boundaries such as 17, 18, 60 and 61 rather than every integer in the world.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-activities",title:"Testing Activities",summary:"From planning through closure",body:`
<h2>Typical testing activities</h2><ol><li><b>Test planning:</b> define scope, objectives, risks, resources, schedule and entry/exit criteria.</li><li><b>Test analysis:</b> examine requirements and identify test conditions.</li><li><b>Test design:</b> create test cases, data and expected outcomes.</li><li><b>Environment preparation:</b> configure hardware, software, accounts and tools.</li><li><b>Test implementation:</b> organize procedures, suites and data.</li><li><b>Execution:</b> run tests and record actual results.</li><li><b>Defect reporting:</b> document reproducible failures and supporting evidence.</li><li><b>Retesting and regression:</b> check fixes and ensure changes have not broken existing behavior.</li><li><b>Closure:</b> summarize results, residual risks, metrics and lessons learned.</li></ol><p>These activities can overlap and may be adapted to the development lifecycle. They are not necessarily a rigid one-way sequence.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-levels",title:"Test Levels",summary:"Unit, integration, system and acceptance testing",body:`
<h2>Common test levels</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Focus</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unit testing</td><td>Individual function, class or component.</td><td>Check the function calculating attendance percentage.</td></tr><tr><td>Integration testing</td><td>Interactions between components or services.</td><td>Check attendance service writes correctly to the database.</td></tr><tr><td>System testing</td><td>Behavior of the complete integrated system.</td><td>Test teacher login, attendance entry, reports and permissions together.</td></tr><tr><td>Acceptance testing</td><td>Whether the system meets user/business acceptance needs.</td><td>College staff confirm the workflow supports their real process.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Levels differ by test object and objective. The same feature may be checked at multiple levels from different perspectives.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-white-black",title:"White-Box and Black-Box Testing",summary:"Internal structure versus externally specified behavior",body:`
<table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>White-box testing</th><th>Black-box testing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Basis</td><td>Internal code or structure.</td><td>Requirements and externally visible behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Uses program structure or logic.</td><td>Does not require knowledge of internal implementation.</td></tr><tr><td>Common techniques</td><td>Statement, branch, path and data-flow testing.</td><td>Equivalence partitioning, boundary analysis, decision tables.</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Ensure both outcomes of an if statement execute.</td><td>Check login accepts valid credentials and rejects invalid ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Limitation</td><td>May miss missing requirements or usability problems.</td><td>May miss unexecuted internal paths or hidden code defects.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>They complement each other. A strong test strategy often combines structural knowledge with requirement-based tests.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-planning",title:"Test Planning and Design",summary:"Define scope, risks, coverage and test cases",body:`
<h2>Test planning</h2><p>Planning determines the purpose and scope of testing, the approach, people, tools, schedule, risks, deliverables and criteria for starting or stopping testing.</p><h2>Test design</h2><p>Design translates requirements and test conditions into specific test cases, data, expected outcomes and coverage targets.</p><ol><li>Study requirements and identify ambiguities.</li><li>Identify test conditions and risks.</li><li>Select appropriate test techniques.</li><li>Design inputs and expected outcomes.</li><li>Prioritize tests based on risk and importance.</li><li>Review test cases for traceability, clarity and feasibility.</li></ol><div class="callout">A test plan answers “what, why, who, when and how broadly?” Test design answers “which concrete cases will check it?”</div>`},
      {id:"u1-monitoring",title:"Monitoring and Measuring Test Execution",summary:"Track progress, outcomes and remaining risk",body:`
<h2>What is monitored?</h2><ul><li>Planned versus executed tests.</li><li>Passed, failed, blocked and not-run tests.</li><li>Defects by severity, status and age.</li><li>Coverage of requirements or code criteria.</li><li>Schedule, effort and remaining risk.</li></ul><h2>Useful measures</h2><p><b>Execution progress</b> can be reported as executed tests divided by planned tests × 100%. <b>Pass rate</b> can be reported as passed tests divided by executed tests × 100%, with the denominator clearly defined. These measures do not by themselves prove quality.</p><div class="callout green"><b>Example:</b> If 80 of 100 planned tests have been executed, execution progress is 80%. If 68 of those 80 passed, the pass rate among executed tests is 85%.</div><p>Metrics need context. A high pass rate may be misleading if tests are weak, important areas are untested, or failed tests are blocked or excluded.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-tools",title:"Test Tools and Automation",summary:"Use tools to improve repeatability and feedback",body:`
<h2>Examples of tool categories</h2><ul><li>Test management and traceability tools.</li><li>Automated UI and API test frameworks.</li><li>Unit-testing frameworks.</li><li>Static analysis and code-quality tools.</li><li>Performance and load-testing tools.</li><li>Defect tracking and reporting tools.</li></ul><h2>Benefits</h2><p>Automation can make repeated regression checks faster, improve repeatability, support data-driven tests and provide rapid feedback.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>Automation requires setup and maintenance. Brittle tests, unstable environments and poor assertions can create false alarms or false confidence. Not every test is worth automating; prioritize stable, repeated, high-value checks.</p><div class="callout">Automation supports testing; it does not replace test design, critical thinking or the need to interpret results.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-team",title:"Test Team Organization and Management",summary:"Roles, independence and communication",body:`
<h2>Typical responsibilities</h2><ul><li><b>Test lead/manager:</b> plans work, coordinates resources, manages risks and reports status.</li><li><b>Test analyst/designer:</b> studies requirements and designs test conditions and cases.</li><li><b>Tester:</b> executes tests, records evidence and reports defects.</li><li><b>Developer:</b> investigates and fixes implementation defects and supports unit testing.</li><li><b>Quality assurance role:</b> helps improve processes and checks adherence to agreed standards.</li></ul><h2>Independence and collaboration</h2><p>Independent testing can reveal assumptions that authors overlook, but complete separation can slow feedback. Effective teams maintain clear responsibilities while communicating closely with developers, product owners and users.</p><p>Organization depends on project size, risk, lifecycle and company structure; the roles above are responsibilities, not mandatory separate people.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-five-views",title:"Five Views of Software Quality",summary:"Different ways to understand quality",body:`
<h2>Five commonly described views</h2><p>Quality can be understood differently by different stakeholders. The following five-view model is commonly presented in software quality discussions; use the precise names and descriptions adopted by your class notes if they differ.</p><table><thead><tr><th>View</th><th>Core idea</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transcendental</td><td>Quality is recognized as excellence, though hard to define precisely.</td><td>A product is perceived as exceptionally well crafted.</td></tr><tr><td>User-based</td><td>Quality is fitness for the user's purpose.</td><td>Teachers can take attendance quickly.</td></tr><tr><td>Manufacturing-based</td><td>Quality is conformance to specifications.</td><td>The implemented fee rules match requirements.</td></tr><tr><td>Product-based</td><td>Quality is tied to measurable product attributes.</td><td>Response time, defect density or reliability measures.</td></tr><tr><td>Value-based</td><td>Quality is considered relative to cost and value.</td><td>The benefits justify the cost of development and operation.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="callout">These views are complementary. A product can conform to its specification but still fail to meet real user needs.</div>`},
      {id:"u1-mccall",title:"McCall’s Quality Factors and Criteria",summary:"Quality factors organized around product use, revision and transition",body:`
<h2>Overview</h2><p>McCall's quality model organizes software quality into factors often grouped around product operation, product revision and product transition. Factors express qualities stakeholders care about; criteria are more specific attributes that help assess those factors.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Quality factors</th><th>Meaning (short)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product operation</td><td>Correctness, Reliability, Efficiency, Integrity, Usability</td><td>How well the product operates for its users.</td></tr><tr><td>Product revision</td><td>Maintainability, Flexibility, Testability</td><td>How readily it can be corrected, adapted and tested.</td></tr><tr><td>Product transition</td><td>Portability, Reusability, Interoperability</td><td>How readily it can move, be reused or work with other systems.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Examples of criteria</h2><ul><li>Correctness may be assessed through traceability to requirements.</li><li>Reliability concerns failure-free operation under stated conditions.</li><li>Usability concerns effort and clarity for users.</li><li>Maintainability concerns the effort needed to diagnose and modify software.</li><li>Portability concerns the effort required to transfer software to another environment.</li><li>Interoperability concerns exchanging information and cooperating with other systems.</li></ul><p>Different textbook presentations may list detailed criteria under factors differently. For an examination, reproduce the exact factor/criterion mapping from the prescribed lecture notes or textbook edition.</p>`},
      {id:"u1-cmm",title:"Capability Maturity Model (CMM)",summary:"Supplementary topic listed under self-learning/team work",supplementary:true,body:`
<h2>Why maturity models exist</h2><p>A process maturity model provides a staged way to describe how consistently an organization defines, manages, measures and improves its processes. The syllabus lists maturity models and the Capability Maturity Model under self-learning/team work, so this page is supplementary rather than a core lecture topic.</p><h2>Classic five-level CMM overview</h2><ol><li><b>Initial:</b> work is ad hoc and success depends heavily on individuals.</li><li><b>Repeatable:</b> basic project management practices make earlier successes more repeatable.</li><li><b>Defined:</b> organization-wide processes are documented and standardized.</li><li><b>Managed:</b> process performance and quality are measured and controlled.</li><li><b>Optimizing:</b> continuous improvement is institutionalized.</li></ol><div class="callout">Use the exact model terminology required by your course. This short overview is included as a clearly marked supplement.</div>`}
    ]},
    {id:2,title:"Unit Testing, Control Flow & Data Flow Testing",subtitle:"Learn testing at code level, then practise structural coverage step by step.",icon:"⌘",topics:[
      {id:"u2-unit-testing",title:"Concept of Unit Testing",summary:"Test a small, isolated program unit",body:`
<h2>Definition</h2><p><b>Unit testing</b> checks a small, individually testable part of software—such as a function, method, class or module—to determine whether it behaves as intended.</p><h2>Why unit testing matters</h2><ul><li>Finds defects close to where they are introduced.</li><li>Provides fast feedback to developers.</li><li>Supports safer refactoring and regression checking.</li><li>Can document expected behavior through executable examples.</li></ul><h2>Example</h2><pre>function attendanceRate(present, total) {\n  if (total === 0) return 0;\n  return (present / total) * 100;\n}</pre><p>Possible tests include present=8,total=10 (80%), present=0,total=10 (0%), and total=0 (the specified special case). Tests should also check invalid inputs if the requirements define their behavior.</p><h2>Drivers and stubs</h2><p>A <b>driver</b> calls the unit under test when the real caller is unavailable. A <b>stub</b> replaces a dependency called by the unit. They help isolate a unit from the rest of the system.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-static",title:"Static Unit Testing",summary:"Inspect artifacts without executing the program",body:`
<h2>Meaning</h2><p>Static testing examines requirements, designs, source code or other work products without running the software. It can include reviews, walkthroughs, inspections and static analysis.</p><h2>What it can reveal</h2><ul><li>Ambiguous or inconsistent requirements.</li><li>Potential null dereferences or unused variables.</li><li>Style violations and suspicious constructs.</li><li>Missing checks and design inconsistencies.</li></ul><h2>Static versus dynamic</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Static testing</th><th>Dynamic testing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Does not execute the target software.</td><td>Executes software with selected inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>Can detect defects early in artifacts.</td><td>Observes runtime behavior and failures.</td></tr><tr><td>Examples: code review, static analyzer.</td><td>Examples: unit test, system test.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>They complement each other; neither alone detects every defect category.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-prevention",title:"Defect Prevention",summary:"Reduce the chance that defects are introduced",body:`
<h2>Meaning</h2><p>Defect prevention uses practices that reduce the likelihood of introducing defects rather than relying only on finding and fixing them later.</p><ul><li>Clear and reviewed requirements.</li><li>Design reviews and coding standards.</li><li>Training and shared guidelines.</li><li>Static analysis and automated checks.</li><li>Root-cause analysis of recurring defects.</li><li>Continuous process improvement.</li></ul><h2>Example</h2><p>If several defects arise from ambiguous date formats, the team can standardize the format, add validation rules and update requirements. Fixing only each individual bug would not address the recurring cause.</p><div class="callout green">Defect detection asks “what went wrong?” Prevention also asks “what in our process allowed this, and how can we stop it recurring?”</div>`},
      {id:"u2-dynamic",title:"Dynamic Unit Testing",summary:"Execute the unit and compare actual with expected behavior",body:`
<h2>Process</h2><ol><li>Identify the unit and its specification.</li><li>Choose representative, boundary and invalid inputs where applicable.</li><li>Define expected results before execution.</li><li>Set up dependencies, stubs or drivers as required.</li><li>Execute the tests.</li><li>Compare actual and expected outcomes.</li><li>Record failures, investigate defects and rerun tests after fixes.</li></ol><p>Dynamic tests can reveal failures only for the paths and conditions exercised. Input selection and coverage therefore matter.</p><h2>Example</h2><p>For a function that accepts marks from 0 to 100, test typical valid values, the boundaries 0 and 100, and invalid values such as -1 and 101 if the specification requires rejection.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-mutation",title:"Mutation Testing",summary:"Check whether tests detect small intentional code changes",body:`
<h2>Idea</h2><p>Mutation testing evaluates the strength of a test suite by making small, deliberate changes to the program. Each changed version is a <b>mutant</b>. If the test suite detects the changed behavior, the mutant is said to be killed; if it survives, the tests may be too weak or the change may be behaviorally equivalent.</p><h2>Example</h2><pre>// Original\nif (age >= 18) allowAccess();\n\n// Mutant: boundary operator changed\nif (age > 18) allowAccess();</pre><p>A test with age=18 should distinguish the original from the mutant. If the suite never tests age=18, the mutant may survive.</p><h2>Mutation score</h2><p>A common form is:</p><div class="callout"><b>Mutation score</b> = killed non-equivalent mutants ÷ total non-equivalent mutants considered × 100%.</div><p>Equivalent mutants must be excluded from the denominator because no test can distinguish them by observable behavior. Mutation testing can be computationally expensive and is not a guarantee of defect-free software.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-debugging",title:"Debugging",summary:"Locate, understand and correct the cause of a failure",body:`
<h2>Testing versus debugging</h2><p>Testing reveals a failure or provides evidence of a defect. Debugging investigates the cause, locates the fault, changes the implementation and checks the correction.</p><h2>Typical debugging steps</h2><ol><li>Reproduce the failure reliably.</li><li>Collect inputs, logs, stack traces and environment details.</li><li>Reduce the case to the smallest useful example.</li><li>Form hypotheses about the root cause.</li><li>Inspect state and execution to isolate the fault.</li><li>Correct the cause rather than masking the symptom.</li><li>Run the original failing test and relevant regression tests.</li></ol><div class="callout">A fix is not complete just because the original symptom disappears; check for side effects and regressions.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-cfg",title:"Control Flow Graph (CFG)",summary:"Represent program execution structure as nodes and directed edges",body:`
<h2>Basic idea</h2><p>A <b>control flow graph (CFG)</b> represents possible control transfers through a program. Nodes represent statements or basic blocks; directed edges represent possible transfers of control. A predicate or decision node has alternative outgoing edges.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><pre>1  read x\n2  if (x > 0)\n3      y = x\n4  else\n5      y = -x\n6  print y</pre><p>For clarity, group the code into nodes: N1 = read x; N2 = decision x &gt; 0; N3 = y=x; N4 = y=-x; N5 = print y.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 620 270" role="img" aria-label="Control flow graph for absolute value example" style="width:100%;max-width:620px;background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;margin:12px 0"><defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#8275f4"/></marker></defs><g stroke="#8275f4" stroke-width="2" fill="none" marker-end="url(#arrow)"><path d="M310 44 L310 70"/><path d="M286 105 L180 140"/><path d="M334 105 L440 140"/><path d="M180 178 L270 218"/><path d="M440 178 L350 218"/></g><g fill="var(--panel)" stroke="#8275f4" stroke-width="2"><rect x="235" y="10" width="150" height="34" rx="9"/><path d="M310 70 L350 91 L310 112 L270 91 Z"/><rect x="110" y="140" width="140" height="38" rx="9"/><rect x="370" y="140" width="140" height="38" rx="9"/><rect x="235" y="218" width="150" height="34" rx="9"/></g><g fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif"><text x="310" y="31">N1: read x</text><text x="310" y="95">N2: x &gt; 0?</text><text x="180" y="163">N3: y = x</text><text x="440" y="163">N4: y = -x</text><text x="310" y="239">N5: print y</text><text x="218" y="120" fill="#8275f4">True</text><text x="402" y="120" fill="#8275f4">False</text></g></svg>
<h2>Edges and paths</h2><p>Edges are N1→N2, N2→N3, N2→N4, N3→N5 and N4→N5. The two complete paths are N1→N2→N3→N5 and N1→N2→N4→N5. One test with x=5 exercises the true branch; a test with x=-3 exercises the false branch.</p><div class="callout green"><b>Graph-building tip:</b> split at decisions and at points where control flow merges. Label nodes consistently, then draw every possible transfer.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-path-criteria",title:"Paths and Path Selection Criteria",summary:"Choose execution paths to satisfy a coverage goal",body:`
<h2>What is a path?</h2><p>A path is a sequence of connected nodes and edges through a control-flow graph. A test input selects a path when the program's decisions cause execution to follow that sequence.</p><h2>Common structural coverage criteria</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Goal</th><th>Important limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Statement / node coverage</td><td>Execute every relevant statement or node at least once.</td><td>May not exercise every decision outcome.</td></tr><tr><td>Branch / edge coverage</td><td>Exercise every relevant edge or branch outcome.</td><td>May not cover every combination of decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Predicate / decision coverage</td><td>Make each decision evaluate to each required outcome, commonly true and false.</td><td>Does not guarantee all paths.</td></tr><tr><td>Path coverage</td><td>Exercise selected paths; all-path coverage attempts every possible path.</td><td>Loops can create infinitely many paths, so all-path coverage is often infeasible.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Exact definitions can vary across course texts. Use the coverage criteria and notation emphasized in the prescribed material.</p><h2>Example</h2><p>In the absolute-value CFG, one positive input covers the true branch and one negative input covers the false branch. Both tests together cover all nodes and branches in this simple acyclic graph.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-test-input",title:"Generating Test Input",summary:"Find inputs that force a desired path",body:`
<h2>Approach</h2><ol><li>Choose the path or branch you want to exercise.</li><li>Translate each decision along the path into a constraint on inputs.</li><li>Combine the constraints.</li><li>Find values satisfying them.</li><li>Check the program's expected output and verify that the intended path is feasible.</li></ol><h2>Example</h2><pre>if (x >= 10)\n    if (x < 20) print("A");\n    else print("B");\nelse print("C");</pre><p>Path to A requires x ≥ 10 and x &lt; 20, so x=15 works. Path to B requires x ≥ 10 and x ≥ 20, so x=20 works. Path to C requires x &lt; 10, so x=9 works.</p><div class="callout">A path is infeasible if its path constraints cannot all be true at the same time. Do not invent a test input for an infeasible path.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-test-selection",title:"Examples of Test Data Selection",summary:"Choose inputs that exercise meaningful behavior",body:`
<h2>Worked example: absolute value</h2><pre>if (x > 0)\n    y = x;\nelse\n    y = -x;\nprint(y);</pre><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Decision</th><th>Expected output</th><th>Coverage contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>x = 5</td><td>True</td><td>5</td><td>True branch</td></tr><tr><td>x = -3</td><td>False</td><td>3</td><td>False branch</td></tr><tr><td>x = 0</td><td>False</td><td>0</td><td>Boundary behavior</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first two tests exercise both decision outcomes. The zero test adds an important boundary case, especially if requirements distinguish zero from negative inputs.</p><h2>Selection principles</h2><ul><li>Cover each required branch or node.</li><li>Include boundary and exceptional values where relevant.</li><li>Choose inputs that distinguish correct behavior from likely faults.</li><li>Keep expected outcomes independent and explicit.</li><li>Record the criterion being claimed; “good coverage” without a defined criterion is vague.</li></ul>`},
      {id:"u2-dataflow-intro",title:"Data Flow Testing: General Idea",summary:"Track where variables are defined and used",body:`
<h2>Idea</h2><p>Data flow testing selects tests by examining how values are defined, changed and used as execution moves through a program. It can reveal problems such as using a variable before assigning a value, assigning a value that is never used, or overwriting a value before its intended use.</p><h2>Definitions and uses</h2><ul><li><b>Definition (def):</b> a statement assigns or otherwise establishes a value for a variable.</li><li><b>Computational use (c-use):</b> a variable's value contributes to a computation, such as <code>total = price * quantity</code>.</li><li><b>Predicate use (p-use):</b> a variable's value affects a condition, such as <code>if (balance &gt; 0)</code>.</li></ul><h2>Why it helps</h2><p>Control-flow coverage asks which statements or branches executed. Data-flow coverage asks whether important relationships between assignments and later uses have been exercised.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-anomaly",title:"Data Flow Anomalies",summary:"Recognize suspicious define/use sequences",body:`
<h2>Typical anomaly patterns</h2><p>Some textbooks use compact symbols such as <b>d</b> (define), <b>u</b> (use) and <b>k</b> (kill or make unavailable). The exact anomaly classification can differ, so match the notation in your lecture material.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Possible concern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Use before definition</td><td>Value may be undefined or stale.</td><td>Print total before assigning it.</td></tr><tr><td>Definition followed by redefinition without use</td><td>First assigned value may be wasted or wrong.</td><td>Set x=3, then x=4 before x is read.</td></tr><tr><td>Definition never used</td><td>Unnecessary computation or missing logic.</td><td>Compute discount but never apply it.</td></tr><tr><td>Use after value becomes invalid</td><td>Lifecycle or state-management bug.</td><td>Use a resource after it has been released.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An apparent anomaly is a signal for investigation, not always proof of a defect. Language rules, control flow and intended behavior matter.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-dynamic-dataflow",title:"Dynamic Data Flow Testing",summary:"Observe definitions and uses along executed paths",body:`
<h2>Overview</h2><p>Dynamic data-flow testing executes a program and observes variable definitions and uses along the paths actually taken. A definition-use (DU) association connects a definition of a variable to a later use reachable without an intervening redefinition that kills that definition.</p><h2>Example</h2><pre>1  x = input()       // def(x)\n2  if (x > 0)        // p-use(x)\n3      y = x + 1     // c-use(x), def(y)\n4  print(y)          // c-use(y)</pre><p>When x&gt;0, the definition at line 1 reaches the predicate use at line 2 and the computational use at line 3. The definition of y at line 3 reaches its use at line 4. When x≤0, line 3 is skipped; then y may be undefined at line 4 unless another path defines it.</p><div class="callout green">For every DU claim, name the variable, definition point, use point and the path that connects them. Check for intervening redefinitions.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-dataflow-graph",title:"Data Flow Graph",summary:"Annotate control-flow nodes with variable definitions and uses",body:`
<h2>Constructing a data-flow representation</h2><ol><li>Draw the program's control-flow graph.</li><li>Identify each variable definition at every node.</li><li>Identify computational uses and predicate uses.</li><li>Trace paths from definitions to reachable uses.</li><li>Stop a definition's path when the variable is redefined, according to the chosen criterion.</li></ol><h2>Example annotations</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Definition</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>x = input()</td><td>x</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>if (x &gt; 0)</td><td>—</td><td>p-use of x</td></tr><tr><td>y = x + 1</td><td>y</td><td>c-use of x</td></tr><tr><td>print(y)</td><td>—</td><td>c-use of y</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some courses call this a data-flow graph when control-flow nodes carry def/use annotations. Preserve the representation used in your lecturer's material.</p>`},
      {id:"u2-dataflow-terms",title:"Data Flow Terms",summary:"Def, use, DU pair, kill and definition-clear path",body:`
<h2>Key terms</h2><ul><li><b>Definition point:</b> a program point where a variable receives a value.</li><li><b>Use point:</b> a program point where the variable's value is read.</li><li><b>c-use:</b> use in a computation or output expression.</li><li><b>p-use:</b> use in a predicate that controls flow.</li><li><b>DU pair:</b> a selected definition and a reachable use for the same variable.</li><li><b>Definition-clear path:</b> a path from a definition to a use with no intervening redefinition of that variable.</li><li><b>Kill:</b> an event that makes a previous definition no longer reach a later use, often through reassignment or scope/lifetime effects.</li></ul><div class="callout">Notation varies between texts. In an exam, define your symbols before using them in a worked graph.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-dataflow-criteria",title:"Data Flow Testing Criteria",summary:"Choose tests to cover definitions, uses and DU associations",body:`
<h2>Common criteria (overview)</h2><p>Data-flow test criteria differ in how much of the definition/use structure they require tests to cover. Your course's exact criteria and formal definitions should be followed from the prescribed notes.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion family</th><th>General goal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All-definitions</td><td>For each relevant definition, exercise at least one reachable use.</td></tr><tr><td>All-uses</td><td>For each relevant definition, exercise the required reachable uses.</td></tr><tr><td>All DU-paths</td><td>Exercise the required definition-clear paths between definitions and uses, subject to the criterion's path-length/feasibility rules.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Terminology and strength ordering can be formalized differently by source, especially around p-uses, c-uses and simple versus loop-containing paths. Do not mix definitions from different conventions in one answer.</p><h2>How to solve a problem</h2><ol><li>List definitions and uses for each variable.</li><li>Find reachable DU associations.</li><li>Exclude paths blocked by redefinitions.</li><li>Check path feasibility.</li><li>Choose test inputs that execute the required associations.</li><li>Report which criterion is satisfied and what remains uncovered.</li></ol>`},
      {id:"u2-compare-criteria",title:"Compare Data Flow Criteria",summary:"How coverage obligations differ",body:`
<h2>Comparison approach</h2><p>When comparing data-flow criteria, explain the coverage obligation rather than only memorizing names.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Question to ask</th><th>Typical work required</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All-definitions</td><td>Has each definition reached at least one required use?</td><td>Choose a path for each definition to a reachable use.</td></tr><tr><td>All-uses</td><td>Have the required uses of each definition been exercised?</td><td>Trace multiple DU associations when a definition reaches multiple uses.</td></tr><tr><td>All DU-paths</td><td>Have required definition-clear paths been exercised?</td><td>Consider alternate paths and path feasibility.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The precise formal obligations must match your syllabus reference. If loops create infinitely many paths, the course criterion may limit which paths count.</p><div class="callout">A strong exam answer states the criterion, defines the obligation, explains how tests are selected, and gives a small example.</div>`},
      {id:"u2-feasible-paths",title:"Feasible Paths and Test Selection",summary:"Separate logically possible paths from executable ones",body:`
<h2>Feasible and infeasible paths</h2><p>A path is <b>feasible</b> if at least one input and program state can cause execution to follow it. It is <b>infeasible</b> if the path constraints contradict one another, so no input can execute it.</p><h2>Example</h2><pre>if (x > 10)\n    if (x < 5) print("impossible");</pre><p>The nested branch requires both x&gt;10 and x&lt;5. These conditions cannot both hold for the same ordinary numeric x, so that path is infeasible.</p><h2>Test selection</h2><ol><li>Write the conditions for each target path.</li><li>Combine them as constraints.</li><li>Solve the constraints.</li><li>If a solution exists, use it as a candidate input and verify execution.</li><li>If no solution exists, document why the path is infeasible rather than claiming it is covered.</li></ol>`},
      {id:"u2-compare-techniques",title:"Comparison of Testing Techniques",summary:"Combine structural, functional and data-flow perspectives",body:`
<table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Main basis</th><th>What it can reveal</th><th>Potential blind spot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Statement coverage</td><td>Executed statements/nodes</td><td>Unexecuted code</td><td>Decision outcomes may be missed.</td></tr><tr><td>Branch coverage</td><td>Decision edges/outcomes</td><td>Untested branches</td><td>Does not cover every path combination.</td></tr><tr><td>Path testing</td><td>Execution paths</td><td>Path-dependent defects</td><td>Loops make all-path coverage impractical.</td></tr><tr><td>Data-flow testing</td><td>Variable definitions and uses</td><td>Undefined, overwritten or unused values; DU-related defects</td><td>Depends on accurate data-flow analysis and selected criteria.</td></tr><tr><td>Black-box testing</td><td>Requirements and input/output behavior</td><td>Missing or incorrect functionality</td><td>May not reveal unexecuted internal logic.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These techniques are complementary. Select them based on the specification, risks, code structure and available time. No single coverage metric guarantees the absence of defects.</p>`}
    ]}
  ],
  questions: [
    {unit:1,type:"2-mark",q:"Define software quality.",a:"Software quality is the degree to which software satisfies stated requirements and user needs under specified conditions. Quality includes attributes such as correctness, reliability, usability, efficiency, security and maintainability."},
    {unit:1,type:"5-mark",q:"Differentiate verification and validation.",a:"Verification checks conformance of work products to specified requirements—“Are we building the product right?” Validation checks whether the product satisfies intended use and user needs—“Are we building the right product?” Reviews and static analysis are common verification activities; executing acceptance or system tests supports validation."},
    {unit:1,type:"5-mark",q:"Explain the relationship between error, fault/defect and failure.",a:"A human error may introduce a fault or defect into a work product. When that fault is activated under particular conditions, it can cause an incorrect state and an observable failure. A fault may remain dormant if the necessary conditions never occur."},
    {unit:1,type:"5-mark",q:"Explain the role and limitations of software testing.",a:"Testing finds defects, checks requirements and business rules, reduces risk, provides evidence for release decisions and builds confidence. However, passing tests cannot generally prove that complex software is defect-free because only a subset of possible inputs, states and paths can be exercised."},
    {unit:1,type:"10-mark",q:"Explain test levels with suitable examples.",a:"Unit testing checks an individual function or component; integration testing checks interactions between components; system testing checks the complete integrated system; acceptance testing checks whether the product meets user or business acceptance needs. Use a college attendance application to illustrate each level and conclude that the levels have different objectives and complement one another."},
    {unit:1,type:"5-mark",q:"What is a test case? List its important elements.",a:"A test case is a documented set of preconditions, inputs, actions and expected results used to check a behavior or requirement. Typical elements include ID, objective, requirement link, preconditions, test data, steps, expected result, actual result and pass/fail status."},
    {unit:1,type:"10-mark",q:"Explain McCall’s software quality model.",a:"McCall’s model groups quality factors into product operation (correctness, reliability, efficiency, integrity, usability), product revision (maintainability, flexibility, testability) and product transition (portability, reusability, interoperability). Explain each factor with a software example and distinguish broad quality factors from more specific evaluation criteria. Follow the exact mapping in the prescribed material."},
    {unit:2,type:"2-mark",q:"Define unit testing.",a:"Unit testing checks an individual, small and testable part of software, such as a function, class or module, to determine whether it behaves as intended."},
    {unit:2,type:"5-mark",q:"Differentiate static and dynamic testing.",a:"Static testing examines work products without executing the target software, for example reviews and static analysis. Dynamic testing executes software with selected inputs and compares actual behavior with expected behavior. Static testing can find issues early; dynamic testing observes runtime behavior. They complement each other."},
    {unit:2,type:"5-mark",q:"What is mutation testing?",a:"Mutation testing evaluates test-suite strength by making small intentional changes (mutants) to a program. A mutant is killed when tests detect the changed behavior. A surviving mutant may indicate weak tests or an equivalent mutant. Mutation score is commonly killed non-equivalent mutants divided by total non-equivalent mutants considered, multiplied by 100%."},
    {unit:2,type:"10-mark",q:"Explain how to construct a control-flow graph and select test inputs.",a:"Identify basic blocks and decisions, create a node for each block, draw directed edges for possible control transfers, label decisions and enumerate relevant paths. Translate the desired path into constraints, solve them to find test inputs, execute the tests, compare outputs with expected results and report the coverage criterion. Mention infeasible paths and loops as limitations."},
    {unit:2,type:"5-mark",q:"Explain statement and branch coverage.",a:"Statement coverage requires each relevant statement or node to execute at least once. Branch coverage requires each relevant decision outcome/edge to execute. Branch coverage is generally stronger than statement coverage for decision outcomes, but neither guarantees all paths or defect detection."},
    {unit:2,type:"5-mark",q:"What is a data-flow anomaly?",a:"A data-flow anomaly is a suspicious pattern in the definition, redefinition, use or invalidation of a variable—for example using a value before it is defined, overwriting a value before it is used, or defining a value that is never used. Such patterns need investigation and are not automatically proof of a defect."},
    {unit:2,type:"10-mark",q:"Explain data-flow testing criteria and definition-use associations.",a:"Identify variable definitions and computational/predicate uses, then trace definition-clear paths from each definition to reachable uses without an intervening redefinition. Select tests to satisfy the chosen criterion, such as all-definitions, all-uses or all DU-paths, according to the formal definitions used in the course. Check feasibility and explain uncovered associations."}
  ],
  quiz: [
    {unit:1,q:"Which question best describes verification?",options:["Does the product meet intended user needs?","Does the work product conform to its specification?","How many users like the interface?","Does the product run on every device?"],answer:1,explain:"Verification checks conformance to specified requirements: are we building the product right?"},
    {unit:1,q:"Which is an observable failure?",options:["A developer misunderstands a requirement","A code review finds a typo","The app displays an incorrect attendance total","A test plan is incomplete"],answer:2,explain:"A failure is observable incorrect behavior during operation."},
    {unit:1,q:"Which is a typical test-case element?",options:["Expected result","Only the developer's opinion","A guaranteed proof of zero defects","An unrelated source file"],answer:0,explain:"Expected results let the tester compare actual behavior with the intended behavior."},
    {unit:2,q:"A control-flow graph edge represents:",options:["A variable type only","A possible transfer of control","A test report title","A quality factor"],answer:1,explain:"Directed edges represent possible control transfers between nodes."},
    {unit:2,q:"A test suite executes both true and false outcomes of a decision. This supports:",options:["Branch/decision coverage","Only code formatting","Mutation equivalence","Acceptance criteria only"],answer:0,explain:"Branch coverage aims to exercise each relevant decision edge/outcome."},
    {unit:2,q:"In data-flow testing, a c-use is:",options:["A use in a computation","A condition outcome only","A code comment","A test environment"],answer:0,explain:"A computational use occurs when a variable's value contributes to a computation or output."},
    {unit:2,q:"What is an infeasible path?",options:["A path executed by every input","A path with no input/state that can execute it","A path with no nodes","Any path with a branch"],answer:1,explain:"Contradictory path constraints make a path infeasible."}
  ]
};